-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell.”\n\nIn this essay, I call that policy-level theory **P-FDT**, for *policy functional decision theory*. The name is not used in the source; it is the assigned name for the policy-selection view indicated in the footnote. The source’s broader discussion of functional decision theory provides the philosophical motivation and the graphical decision-theoretic framework, but the explicit formalization of P-FDT itself is very thin. The aim here is therefore reconstructive: I will give a mathematical formulation of P-FDT that is faithful to the footnote, explain how it evaluates policies, and identify where the source leaves important choices open. Where I add assumptions, I will label them as my own.\n\nP-FDT’s central claim is that the proper object of rational choice is not an isolated action but a *policy*: a function from observations, or more generally decision-relevant inputs, to actions. The agent asks which policy, if output by her decision function, would produce the best expected outcome. She then applies the chosen policy to her actual input.\n\n## 2. The object of choice: policies rather than acts\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal X\\) be a set of possible observation histories or inputs, and let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a finite set of actions. A policy is a function\n\n\\[\n\\pi : \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A.\n\\]\n\nIn a sequential problem, \\(\\mathcal X\\) should be understood as the set of histories available at decision points, so a policy is a complete strategy. Let \\(\\Pi\\) denote the set of admissible policies.\n\nThe agent has a world-model \\(M\\), which in the paper’s graphical style is represented by a probability distribution \\(P\\) together with dependency information \\(G\\). The dependency information is not merely causal: it is meant to encode all decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies, including logical and computational dependencies between the agent’s decision function and predictors, copies, or other agents’ models of her.\n\nP-FDT introduces a distinguished node in this model. Let\n\n\\[\nS = \\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}(\\underline{M})\n\\]\n\nbe the variable representing the policy output by the agent’s policy-selection function when run on her model \\(M\\). The underlining indicates quotation or dequoting: the node refers to the agent’s own model and algorithm. This self-reference is not assumed to require the agent to know the value of \\(S\\) in advance; it only requires that the model contain a variable representing that value.\n\nP-FDT then evaluates candidate policies by intervening on this node. The expected utility of a policy \\(\\pi\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{EU}_{\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}}(\\pi)\n\\;:=\\;\n\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[V \\mid do(S=\\pi)\\right],\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(V\\) is the utility variable and \\(do(\\cdot)\\) is an intervention operator analogous to Pearl’s causal \\(do\\)-operator, but applied to a logical/computational variable rather than a purely physical action variable.\n\nThe P-FDT recommendation is then\n\n\\[\n\\pi^*\n\\in\n\\arg\\max_{\\pi \\in \\Pi}\n\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[V \\mid do(S=\\pi)\\right].\n\\]\n\nIf the agent’s actual input is \\(x \\in \\mathcal X\\), the action taken is\n\n\\[\na^* = \\pi^*(x).\n\\]\n\nThis is the core of P-FDT. The theory does not first ask, “What action should I take now?” It asks, “What policy should this decision function output?” The action is then obtained by applying that policy to the actual observations.\n\nThis contrasts with the three more familiar rules discussed in the source:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{EDT}(P,x)\n=\n\\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb{E}[V \\mid X=x, A=a],\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CDT}(P,G,x)\n=\n\\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb{E}[V \\mid do(A=a), X=x],\n\\]\n\nand the action-level functional decision rule presented in the main text,\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{FDT}_{\\text{action}}(P,G,x)\n=\n\\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb{E}[V \\mid do(\\mathrm{FDT}(\\underline{P},\\underline{G},\\underline{x})=a)].\n\\]\n\nP-FDT replaces the last equation with a policy-level choice:\n\n\\[\n\\pi^*\n=\n\\arg\\max_{\\pi \\in \\Pi}\n\\mathbb{E}[V \\mid do(\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}(\\underline{M})=\\pi)],\n\\qquad\na^* = \\pi^*(x).\n\\]\n\nThe difference is subtle in one-shot problems. If the policy domain has only one possible input, policies collapse to actions. But once observations are possible, and especially once predictors or other agents respond to the agent’s disposition, the policy is the natural object of prediction and control.\n\n## 3. Subjunctive dependence and policy counterfactuals\n\nThe source motivates functional decision theory through the notion of *subjunctive dependence*. Two calculators computing the same arithmetic problem on opposite sides of the world are not causally connected, but their outputs are not merely statistically correlated either. If they are functioning correctly, then their outputs depend on the same mathematical function. In the same way, an agent and a reliable predictor of the agent may both depend on the output of the same decision algorithm: the agent by executing it, the predictor by modeling it.\n\nP-FDT extends this idea from actions to policies. Suppose a predictor has modeled the agent’s policy-selection procedure. Then the predictor’s behavior may depend not only on what action the agent will take in one actual circumstance, but on the policy the agent will implement. If the agent’s policy says “one-box when the box appears full; two-box when it appears empty,” the predictor can predict the action conditional on each possible observation. If the agent’s policy says “refuse all blackmail demands,” potential blackmailers can predict that blackmail will fail.\n\nThe relevant counterfactual is therefore not primarily:\n\n> What would happen if my physical action were different?\n\nInstead it is:\n\n> What would happen if the policy output by this very decision function were different?\n\nThis is a counterpossible or counterlogical condition in the strict sense: if the agent’s actual decision function in fact outputs \\(\\pi^*\\), then there is no genuinely possible world in which it outputs a different \\(\\pi\\) on the same inputs. Nevertheless, the agent must reason as if the output of the function could vary, while holding fixed the relevant logical, mathematical, causal, and statistical structure of the world. The source admits that this requires an undeveloped theory of counterpossible reasoning. P-FDT inherits that difficulty.\n\nOperationally, we can represent the needed counterfactuals with a graph \\(G\\). The graph contains a node \\(S\\) for the policy output. Variables that subjunctively depend on the agent’s policy have arrows from \\(S\\). Variables merely statistically correlated with the agent’s behavior do not. Thus:\n\n- A reliable predictor of the agent’s policy is downstream of \\(S\\).\n- A copy of the agent running the same policy-selection algorithm is linked to \\(S\\).\n- A carcinogenic lesion that causes both smoking and cancer is not downstream of \\(S\\).\n- Causal consequences of the action are downstream of the action node, which is itself determined by the policy and the observation.\n\nUnder \\(do(S=\\pi)\\), the policy node is set to \\(\\pi\\). The agent’s action becomes \\(A=\\pi(X)\\), where \\(X\\) may itself be affected by predictor behavior. All variables that depend on \\(S\\) are updated accordingly; variables that do not depend on \\(S\\) are held fixed. Expected utility is then computed in the resulting hypothetical model.\n\nThis is the policy-level analogue of the paper’s statement that functional decision theory preserves decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies, whereas EDT preserves too many correlations and CDT preserves too few.\n\n## 4. How P-FDT evaluates policies\n\nA usable P-FDT procedure can be described as follows.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the policy space.**  \nSpecify the inputs \\(\\mathcal X\\) over which the policy is defined. These may be observations, observation histories, or information states. Specify the available actions \\(\\mathcal A\\). The policy space is \\(\\Pi = \\mathcal A^{\\mathcal X}\\), possibly restricted to computationally or practically admissible policies.\n\n**Step 2: Build a world-model with a policy node.**  \nRepresent the agent’s beliefs by \\(P\\). Add a variable \\(S\\) denoting the output of \\(\\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}(\\underline{M})\\). Add structural dependencies showing how \\(S\\) affects the agent’s own action, predictors, copies, and other agents’ models. Add ordinary causal dependencies among physical variables.\n\n**Step 3: Evaluate each candidate policy by intervention.**  \nFor each \\(\\pi \\in \\Pi\\), construct the hypothetical distribution\n\n\\[\nP(\\cdot \\mid do(S=\\pi)).\n\\]\n\nUnder this intervention, the agent’s action at input \\(x\\) is \\(\\pi(x)\\). If predictors respond to the policy, their predictions change. If observations depend on predictions, the distribution over observations may also change.\n\nThe expected utility is\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{EU}(\\pi)\n=\n\\sum_{v} v \\, P(V=v \\mid do(S=\\pi)).\n\\]\n\nIn a sequential setting, this can be written more dynamically. Let \\(u\\) range over exogenous uncertainty, and let \\(\\tau_\\pi(u)\\) be the trajectory or terminal outcome induced by policy \\(\\pi\\) under \\(u\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{EU}(\\pi)\n=\n\\mathbb{E}_{u}\\left[U(\\tau_\\pi(u))\\right],\n\\]\n\nwhere the expectation is taken under the policy intervention.\n\n**Step 4: Choose the best policy.**  \nSelect\n\n\\[\n\\pi^* \\in \\arg\\max_{\\pi \\in \\Pi} \\mathrm{EU}(\\pi).\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Apply the policy to the actual input.**  \nIf the actual observation is \\(x\\), execute\n\n\\[\na^* = \\pi^*(x).\n\\]\n\nThis procedure makes clear why P-FDT is not simply EDT with a different name. EDT conditions on the action \\(A=a\\), thereby preserving all evidential correlations between the action and the world. P-FDT intervenes on the policy node, thereby preserving dependencies that track the policy function while breaking merely statistical correlations.\n\nIt is also not CDT. CDT intervenes on the physical action node \\(A\\), holding fixed predictors and other variables not causally downstream of the action. P-FDT intervenes upstream of the physical action, on the policy selected by the decision function. This allows the intervention to change predictors’ predictions, copies’ behavior, and even the probability of receiving certain observations.\n\nA subtle point concerns conditioning on the actual observation. The source says that functional decision theorists respond to observations not by ordinary Bayesian conditioning alone, but by changing the node on which they intervene. In the action-level formulation, the agent intervenes on \\(\\mathrm{FDT}(P,G,x)\\). In P-FDT, the natural interpretation is that the agent chooses a policy over possible inputs and then applies it to the actual input. If the actual input is itself affected by the policy, P-FDT should not treat that input as ordinary evidence that overrides the policy choice. This is especially clear in transparent Newcomb problems and blackmail cases: the fact that the agent sees a full box or receives a blackmail letter may itself be evidence that the policy has certain properties.\n\nThe source does not fully specify which observations, if any, may be conditioned on as policy-independent evidence. My own operational proposal is this: P-FDT may condition on evidence that is plausibly independent of the policy node, but it should not condition away the policy-relevant dependencies that make predictors responsive to the policy. In practice, this means distinguishing between:\n\n1. **Policy-dependent observations**, such as seeing a full box because a predictor predicted the policy; and  \n2. **Policy-independent observations**, such as learning background facts that do not depend on the agent’s decision procedure.\n\nP-FDT treats the first as inputs to the policy, not as reasons to abandon policy evaluation. It may update on the second.\n\n## 5. Examples\n\n### 5.1 Newcomb’s problem\n\nIn standard Newcomb’s problem, a reliable predictor fills box B iff she predicts the agent will one-box. The agent chooses between one-boxing and two-boxing.\n\nHere the policy space is trivial: there is effectively one observation, so policies correspond to actions. Let \\(\\pi_1\\) be the one-boxing policy and \\(\\pi_2\\) the two-boxing policy. Suppose the predictor is \\(99\\%\\) accurate.\n\nIf \\(S=\\pi_1\\), the predictor probably predicted one-boxing, so box B is full with probability \\(0.99\\). Expected utility is approximately\n\n\\[\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000\n+\n0.01 \\cdot \\$0\n=\n\\$990{,}000.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(S=\\pi_2\\), the predictor probably predicted two-boxing, so box B is empty with probability \\(0.99\\). Expected utility is approximately\n\n\\[\n0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000\n+\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000\n=\n\\$11{,}000.\n\\]\n\nP-FDT chooses \\(\\pi_1\\), and the agent one-boxes.\n\nThis reproduces the source’s verdict for Newcomb’s problem, but the object of intervention is explicitly the policy node.\n\n### 5.2 The smoking lesion problem\n\nIn the smoking lesion problem, smoking is correlated with cancer only because a lesion causes both smoking and cancer. Smoking itself does not affect cancer.\n\nA policy to smoke or refrain does not subjunctively affect whether the agent has the lesion. In the graphical model, the policy node influences the action but not the lesion or cancer variables. Therefore, under \\(do(S=\\pi_{\\text{smoke}})\\), the probability of cancer remains the same as under \\(do(S=\\pi_{\\text{refrain}})\\), while smoking adds a direct utility benefit.\n\nThus P-FDT chooses the smoking policy. This matches the source’s claim that a functional decision theorist can one-box in Newcomb’s problem and smoke in the smoking lesion problem without switching theories.\n\nThe important point is that P-FDT does not treat the correlation between smoking and cancer as a reason to refrain. The lesion is not computing the agent’s policy. It is not subjunctively dependent on the output of the decision function.\n\n### 5.3 Transparent Newcomb and Parfit’s hitchhiker\n\nIn transparent Newcomb’s problem, the agent sees whether box B is full. The predictor filled B iff she predicted that the agent would one-box upon seeing that both boxes are full. In Parfit’s hitchhiker problem, a driver helps the agent iff she predicts that the agent will pay after being rescued.\n\nThese cases show why policies are natural. The agent’s behavior must be specified for different possible observations: seeing a full box or an empty box, being in the city or still in the desert.\n\nLet \\(\\pi_C\\) be the policy:\n\n\\[\n\\pi_C(\\text{full}) = \\text{one-box}, \\qquad\n\\pi_C(\\text{empty}) = \\text{two-box}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\pi_D\\) be the policy:\n\n\\[\n\\pi_D(\\text{full}) = \\text{two-box}, \\qquad\n\\pi_D(\\text{empty}) = \\text{two-box}.\n\\]\n\nAssume the predictor is \\(99\\%\\) accurate.\n\nIf \\(S=\\pi_C\\), the predictor probably predicted that the agent would one-box upon seeing a full box. Then with probability \\(0.99\\), B is full and the agent sees a full box, one-boxes, and receives \\(\\$1{,}000{,}000\\). With probability \\(0.01\\), B is empty and the agent sees an empty box, two-boxes, and receives \\(\\$1{,}000\\). Expected utility is approximately\n\n\\[\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000\n+\n0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}000\n=\n\\$990{,}010.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(S=\\pi_D\\), the predictor probably predicted two-boxing. Then B is empty with probability \\(0.99\\), and the agent receives \\(\\$1{,}000\\). With probability \\(0.01\\), the predictor mistakenly fills B; the agent sees a full box, two-boxes, and receives \\(\\$1{,}001{,}000\\). Expected utility is approximately\n\n\\[\n0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000\n+\n0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000\n=\n\\$11{,}000.\n\\]\n\nP-FDT therefore selects \\(\\pi_C\\). If the agent actually sees a full box, she one-boxes. She does this even though, conditional on seeing a full box, taking both boxes appears locally dominant. The policy that includes one-boxing upon seeing a full box is what makes the box likely to be full.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to Parfit’s hitchhiker. The policy “pay if rescued” is predicted by the driver and therefore causes rescue; the policy “refuse if rescued” does not. P-FDT chooses the paying policy and then pays upon reaching the city.\n\n### 5.4 Why policies matter in multi-agent settings\n\nThe footnote says that the policy/action distinction “only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell,” but it does not give a detailed multi-agent example. We can nonetheless see why policies are important.\n\nIn many strategic interactions, other agents do not merely predict one action; they predict a whole strategy. They ask: What will this agent do if threatened? What will she do if she observes cooperation? What will she do in off-equilibrium branches? A policy is the object that answers these questions.\n\nConsider an extortion environment. Many possible extortionists can observe or predict an agent’s decision procedure. Each will send a blackmail demand iff they predict the agent’s policy would pay upon receiving the demand. If the agent pays, she loses \\(\\$1{,}000\\); if she refuses and the damaging process is already running, she may face a much larger loss. Locally, after receiving a demand, paying may look attractive. But if the agent’s policy is “pay when demanded,” she becomes a target. If her policy is “refuse when demanded,” the demand is usually never sent.\n\nP-FDT evaluates the policy globally. The policy \\(\\pi_R\\) with\n\n\\[\n\\pi_R(\\text{demand}) = \\text{refuse}\n\\]\n\nmay have high expected utility because it deters demands. The policy \\(\\pi_P\\) with\n\n\\[\n\\pi_P(\\text{demand}) = \\text{pay}\n\\]\n\nmay have lower expected utility because it invites demands. P-FDT selects \\(\\pi_R\\). If a demand nevertheless arrives because of predictor error, P-FDT still executes \\(\\pi_R(\\text{demand}) = \\text{refuse}\\).\n\nThe source’s action-level formulation can already give this verdict in some blackmail cases by imagining that refusing changes the probability of receiving the letter. The policy formulation makes the reasoning cleaner and more stable: the agent does not ask what to do after the demand in isolation. She asks what policy to embody, because other agents respond to the policy.\n\nIn multi-agent games more generally, P-FDT can represent each agent as having a policy node. If agents are copies of one another, or if they have accurate models of one another’s policy-selection functions, then one agent’s policy node may be subjunctively correlated with another’s. This opens the door to cooperative behavior in prisoner’s dilemmas, voting coalitions, and coordination problems. The source mentions such cases, but does not provide a full game-theoretic solution concept for the policy version. That is an open area.\n\n## 6. Open questions and operational assumptions\n\nThe footnote gives the core idea of P-FDT but leaves several important questions unresolved. I list the main ones here and indicate the operational interpretations I find most reasonable.\n\n### 6.1 When is the policy selected?\n\nP-FDT says the agent chooses a policy and then applies it to her inputs. But in real problems, the agent may find herself already inside a sequence of observations. Should the policy be selected at the beginning of the problem, at the current moment, or at the earliest point where predictors could have modeled her?\n\nThe source’s examples suggest an updateless or early-selection interpretation. In transparent Newcomb, the predictor fills the box only if she predicts the agent will one-box upon seeing a full box. If the agent selected her policy only after seeing the full box, the predictor’s prediction would already be fixed. The successful policy must be treated as the disposition the predictor anticipated.\n\nMy proposed operational rule is this: P-FDT should select policies from the perspective that is relevant to the predictors and dependencies in the problem. If predictors depend on the agent’s policy before the current observation, the agent should act according to the policy that would have been best from that earlier perspective. If new policy-independent information arrives, the agent may update the model and perhaps reselect a policy, but she should not allow policy-dependent observations to break the predictor-relevant commitment.\n\nThis is not explicitly settled by the source.\n\n### 6.2 What counts as the policy’s input?\n\nThe policy maps inputs to actions, but the source does not say how to choose \\(\\mathcal X\\). In a simple problem, \\(\\mathcal X\\) might be “see full box” or “see empty box.” In a sequential problem, it might be full histories. In a multi-agent problem, it might include observations of other agents’ predicted policies.\n\nA reasonable convention is to let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be the set of decision-relevant information states that the agent distinguishes and that other agents can predict she may occupy. The policy should specify what the agent would do in each such state, including states that might not occur if the policy is predicted accurately.\n\n### 6.3 How should P-FDT handle actual evidence?\n\nP-FDT cannot simply condition on every observation, because some observations are downstream of the policy. If the agent receives a blackmail letter, conditioning on the letter can make paying look like good news. But P-FDT asks whether the policy that refuses letters would have prevented the letter.\n\nI proposed above that P-FDT should distinguish policy-dependent from policy-independent evidence. This distinction is not formalized in the source. It is a necessary addition if P-FDT is to be applied to real problems.\n\n### 6.4 How are subjunctive dependencies learned?\n\nThe source openly says that we lack a Pearl-style account of how to infer subjunctive dependencies from observation and experiment. CDT can appeal to causal discovery; P-FDT needs a broader account of logical, computational, and counterfactual dependencies.\n\nThis is perhaps the largest open problem for P-FDT. In the examples, we simply assume the correct graph: predictors depend on the agent’s decision function, lesions do not, copies depend on the same computation, and so on. A practical P-FDT agent would need heuristics for recognizing when another system is modeling her policy or implementing a relevantly similar algorithm.\n\n### 6.5 What is the multi-agent solution concept?\n\nThe footnote says the policy/action distinction matters mainly in multi-agent dilemmas, but it does not say exactly how. One possibility is that each P-FDT agent unilaterally selects a policy by intervening on her own policy node while representing other agents’ policy nodes probabilistically. If agents are symmetric or share algorithms, the model may include logical correlations between their policy nodes. In the limit, this might require solving fixed points over policy choices.\n\nThis is not supplied by the source. My own view is that P-FDT should not automatically be identified with Nash equilibrium or any other standard game-theoretic solution concept. It is better seen as a rule for individual policy selection under beliefs about subjunctive dependence. In symmetric cases, that rule may produce cooperative outcomes similar to superrationality; in asymmetric cases, it may resemble commitment-based strategic reasoning. But the formal game theory remains to be developed.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nPolicy functional decision theory takes the core functionalist insight of the source paper and applies it to the object that predictors and other agents often actually care about: the agent’s policy. Instead of asking which physical action to perform in isolation, P-FDT asks which policy the agent’s decision function should output. It evaluates policies by intervening on a policy node in a world-model that includes subjunctive dependencies, then applies the selected policy to the actual input.\n\nIn standard Newcomb problems, this yields one-boxing. In smoking lesion problems, it yields smoking. In transparent Newcomb and Parfit’s hitchhiker, it yields the seemingly counterintuitive but utility-maximizing choices: one-box upon seeing a full box and pay after being rescued. In blackmail and multi-agent settings, the policy formulation gives a clear account of why agents should choose dispositions that deter exploitation, even when local action-by-action reasoning favors capitulation.\n\nThe source leaves much unfinished. It does not fully formalize the policy version, does not specify how to condition on evidence, and does not develop the multi-agent game theory that the footnote says is where policies matter most. The reconstruction given here is therefore partly interpretive. But the central normative idea is clear: rational choice is not merely the selection of an act, but the selection of a policy from the global perspective of the world that policy would bring about.\n\n## Reference\n\nYudkowsky, E., and Soares, N. “Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality.” arXiv:1710.05060v2.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need answer user's request: write substantive mathematical essay ~2400-3000 words explaining P-FDT, taking policy-selection passage in footnote 16 as primary source for P-FDT. Need use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples, identify other decision theories by distinct names. Keep quotations faithful. Explain best mathematical understanding and application. Where footnote leaves choices unresolved, explain uncertainties and additional assumptions. Distinguish source claims from own proposals. Need likely produce final essay only. Need think deeply.\n\nWe need parse source. Footnote 16: \"As mentioned earlier, the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action. The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell, so we have set that distinction aside in this paper for ease of exposition.\" Also earlier footnote 7: \"In the authors' preferred formalization of FDT, agents actually iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions. This makes a difference in certain multi-agent dilemmas, but will not make a difference in this paper.\"\n\nUser says: \"Take the policy-selection passage in footnote 16 of the supplied paper as the primary source for P-FDT in this assignment.\" So P-FDT stands for policy functional decision theory. Need explain P-FDT based on policy intervention rather than action intervention. Need maybe rename FDT to P-FDT? Use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples. Need identify other decision theories (CDT, EDT, maybe action-level FDT? Could call it action-FDT? But source calls FDT; user says use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, and identify any other decision theory by distinct name. Need be careful: source's FDT in main text is action-intervention version? Footnote says preferred formulation intervenes to choose policy. We need treat P-FDT as policy version. We can refer to source's main-text FDT as \"action-level FDT\" or \"the paper's action-level presentation\" but maybe not call it FDT? Since assigned theory is P-FDT, we can say the source calls the general approach FDT but footnote's preferred formulation is policy-selection; I'll use P-FDT for that policy-level theory. Distinguish claims: source claims policy formulation differs in multi-agent dilemmas, but not elaborated. Need develop operational interpretation.\n\nNeed essay ~2400-3000 words including references and any material. We can include equations. Need central definitions, reasoning, implications. Need explain how theory evaluates alternative actions or policies, relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where source leaves unresolved, explain uncertainty and propose operational interpretation. Need likely include examples: Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb, maybe multi-agent (matching pennies? prisoner's dilemma? voting coalition? game where policies matter). Need mathematical definition of P-FDT: choose policy π mapping observations/history to actions to maximize expected utility under intervention on node representing output of P-FDT function as policy. Then actual action = π(obs). Need compare with EDT, CDT. Need formalism: world model (P,G) with variables including policy-output variable Π = P-FDT(P,G) or π*, perhaps policy variable is mapping. Intervention do(Π=π) sets agent's behavior rule; observations may be upstream? Need careful: In action-FDT, observation changes intervention node: FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_a E[V | do(FDT(P,G,x)=a)]. In policy-FDT, maybe choose policy π: P-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(P-FDT(P,G)=π)] then execute π(x). Since policy maps all possible observations to actions, the intervention on policy can affect which observations occur? In mechanical blackmail, if policy refuses when letter, predictor doesn't send letter; actual observation letter then under policy-refuse has counterfactual lower probability of letter. But if actual observation is letter, do we still evaluate policy globally before conditioning on actual obs? Footnote says choose policy, then apply inputs to select action. It likely means updateless: select policy based on prior, not condition on current observation? The source's main action-FDT still changes intervention node by observation but imagines different observations counterfactually in mechanical blackmail? It says FDT says agents should entertain hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have made different observations. In footnote 16 policy version likely resolves this by choosing policy at outset over observation-action mappings. Need define P-FDT as policy selection: at decision time, with model and maybe current observation, choose policy? If current observation known, selecting policy after observation may be equivalent to action selection for current observation unless policy commitments affect predictor's prior predictions? In transparent Newcomb, need choose policy for seeing full/empty before predictor predicts. If agent at observation full chooses policy for all possible observations, can one-box due to global policy. But if policy chosen only after seeing full? Hmm.\n\nNeed infer P-FDT from source. Footnote 16: \"the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" So node FDT(-) output is a policy. Inputs? maybe P,G? It chooses policy; then agent applies her inputs (observations) to policy to select action. In graph, intervention on FDT node sets policy. The policy variable determines action given observation. If observations are endogenous and affected by predictor, the policy affects observations through predictor? For selection, we maximize expected utility over policies ex ante? Equation could be:\n\nP-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(Π = π)], where Π denotes P-FDT(P,G). Then given actual observation x, action = π(x).\n\nIf observations arrive sequentially, perhaps at each decision point choose policy over future observations? Or choose policy as soon as possible, before predictor? Need operational interpretation: P-FDT selects the policy at the earliest relevant point, not conditioned on actual observation except as input to policy. If no binding precommitment, the agent's disposition is the policy. At observation x, P-FDT executes π*(x), where π* chosen by global policy evaluation. If a new decision problem arises not covered by prior policy, P-FDT selects the policy that would have been optimal from an appropriate prior/updateless standpoint, maybe using current model but not conditioning on contingencies that are subjunctively affected by policy. Need discuss unresolved: Which observations are inputs? What if policy selection occurs after predictor? How to define equivalence classes of agents/policies? How to handle self-reference and logical uncertainty? Need propose reasonable operational interpretation: Use a decision situation's \"policy node\" representing the output of the agent's decision algorithm for that situation, mapping all observation histories that could arise under the situation to actions. Evaluate candidate policies by intervening on this node in a structural model that includes predictors/other agents as functions of the policy (or its outputs). Choose policy maximizing expected utility with respect to prior over exogenous variables. Then action is policy(current observation). If forced to choose after observation, still use policy selected by pre-observation/counterfactual evaluation; if model changes substantially, reselect policy but include dependencies of predictors on the policy-selection algorithm, not just current action. Need perhaps call this \"policy node\" and \"policy intervention.\"\n\nNeed keep quotations faithful if quote footnote. Could quote: \"intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" Need perhaps quote \"The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell\". Also footnote 7: \"iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions.\" Use P-FDT. Need not over quote.\n\nNeed mathematical precision: Let \\mathcal{X} observation histories, \\mathcal{A} actions, policies \\Pi = \\mathcal{X}\\to\\mathcal{A} (maybe finite). Let world model include exogenous variables U, observations X, action A=π(X), outcome O, utility V. The policy-selection node S outputs π. Intervention do(S=π) sets the agent's decision rule. The expected utility of policy π:\n\nEU^P(π) = E[V | do(S=π)]\n\nwhere expectation with respect to P under intervention. P-FDT chooses\n\nπ^* ∈ argmax_{π∈Π} EU^P(π),\n\nthen outputs a = π^*(x) for actual input x.\n\nIf the agent has already received x, a policy-level theory may still compare policies by their ex ante expected utility, not conditional on x, if predictors depend on the policy. However, sometimes actual x provides information about exogenous variables not affected by policy. Need decide if P-FDT conditions on x? Source action-FDT: FDT(P,G,obs) changes intervention node to FDT(P,G,obs), but doesn't condition? It says FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon, not Bayesian conditionalization. In transparent Newcomb they evaluate FDT(P_f,G_f,full) with intervention on FDT(...,full), but expected utility calculation includes possibility that if output twobox, predictor predicted twobox and box empty, even though actual observed full. They do condition on Obs=full? There is a typo: E(V | do(FDTpgv{full}=act, Obs=full) maybe? They said evaluates FDT(Pf,Gf,full), finding act maximizing E(V | do(FDTpgv{full}=act, Obs=full). But in calculation, if output one-box and accurate, full; if inaccurate, empty and action determined by FDT(empty). They condition on actual Obs=full? If condition on Obs=full, intervention on FDT(full) affecting prediction might change probability of Obs? Need maybe they condition only as selecting node, not conditioning distribution? The text has \"Fiona does not react to observations by conditioning her distribution. Rather, she reacts to observations by switching which variable she intervenes on\". The displayed formula has Obs=full but maybe typo. Need interpret P-FDT: no conditioning on actual observation if observation is downstream of policy? But actual observation is input to policy; policy must be selected given input? Hmm.\n\nFor policy version: choose policy π; observation x is then an input to π. If actual observation is x, we choose π? If we maximize ex ante EU over all X, π may choose an action at x that is not locally optimal given x but supports favorable counterfactuals. Transparent Newcomb: policy π(full)=onebox, π(empty)=twobox maybe. Ex ante EU high. At actual full, execute onebox. If we selected policy after seeing full but only for current branch? Could choose π where π(full)=onebox and arbitrary elsewhere; predictor predicted policy? If predictor predicts policy, one-box still. But if policy selection occurs after box is filled, predictor can't depend on it? Yet source says one-box even when sees full because predictor predicted that agent would one-box upon seeing full. That requires policy/disposition existed before prediction. P-FDT should treat the agent's policy as the thing predicted, not selected after observation. Thus operational: policy selection is updateless or at earliest point before predictors; actual observation just indexes into policy.\n\nNeed discuss multi-agent difference. Footnote says policy matters in multi-agent dilemmas. Need construct example where action-level vs policy-level differ. Perhaps game where another agent predicts your policy, not just action? Or Newcomb with predictor predicts what you'd do in each state; policy can commit to respond to observations in a way that affects predictor's behavior. Multi-agent: matching pennies? If both choose policies, equilibrium? Need illustrate why policy is needed: In games, selecting an action at each information set can be time-inconsistent; policy selection can commit to strategies (e.g., in Prisoner's Dilemma against a copy, choose cooperate policy). But action-FDT also cooperates. Need a case where policy vs action FDT differ: maybe \"Newcomb with a threat\": predictor punishes if your policy would choose A in some unobserved counterfactual? Or multi-agent where other player's action depends on your entire strategy, not only current action. Example: an extortioner demands payment iff your policy would pay in a counterfactual branch; P-FDT can choose a policy of refusing in all branches, making extortion unprofitable. But action-FDT in blackmail already refuses by imagining policy-like? Footnote says difference only in multi-agent. Could be game-theoretic scenarios where multiple agents each choose policies that depend on each other's policies, e.g., bargaining, threat, commitment. Need not have exact source example; can develop plausible.\n\nNeed explain how P-FDT evaluates alternative actions or policies. It evaluates policies, not individual actions. For a given policy π, construct hypothetical world where decision function outputs π; all variables that subjunctively depend on that output (predictors, copies, other agents modeling you) are updated accordingly. Then compute expected utility. Choose π with highest EU. Actual action = π(obs). This means alternative actions are evaluated via policies: action a at observation x is assessed as part of π where π(x)=a and with specified behavior in other possible observations. If several policies share π(x)=a but differ elsewhere, their expected utilities can differ; P-FDT chooses best overall policy, not best local action. Thus actions inherit value from policy. Need perhaps define projection: The P-FDT action at x is π^*(x). If multiple optimal policies disagree at x, tie-breaking unresolved; propose choose any, perhaps arbitrary or lexicographic. Need discuss.\n\nNeed relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Source's subjunctive dependence: systems computing same function have outputs logically constrained. P-FDT preserves decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies under policy intervention: predictors of the policy, copies of agent, other agents' models. It severs mere statistical correlations (lesion) and preserves causal downstream effects. Counterfactuals are counterpossible: if P-FDT output different policy, predictors would have predicted differently. Need need graph G encodes how changing policy node affects variables. Equation analogous to Pearl do but on policy variable. Need note uncertainty: no formal True operator, no method to learn subjunctive graph; source leaves unresolved. For P-FDT, even harder: how to represent policies, identify which function is \"this very policy-selection function,\" determine equivalence across agents, choose domain of observations. Need develop operational assumptions: modeler supplies structural graph with policy node; we assume finite observation/action spaces; logical uncertainty represented probabilistically; predictors are functions of policy node or its outputs; self-reference handled by fixed-point/quine. Need distinguish own proposal.\n\nNeed maybe define P-FDT equation:\n\nLet \\Phi be set of admissible policies. Let S be a variable denoting P-FDT(P,G) (or P-FDT(M)). Then\n\nP-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Φ} E[V | do(S=π)].\n\nIf observations are inputs, the chosen action after input x is\n\nA = P-FDT(P,G)(x).\n\nFor sequential decisions, policies map histories to actions; EU is sum over histories:\n\nEU(π) = \\sum_{u} P(u) V(τ_π(u)) perhaps where τ_π is trajectory induced by π and world dynamics under intervention.\n\nCould write:\n\nEU^P(π)=\\sum_{o,a,v} P(o,a,v | do(S=π)) U(v).\n\nNeed compare to EDT:\n\nEDT(P,x)=argmax_a E[V | X=x, A=a]\n\nCDT:\n\nCDT(P,G,x)=argmax_a E[V | do(A=a), X=x? maybe]\n\nAction-level FDT from source:\n\nFDT_act(P,G,x)=argmax_a E[V | do(FDT(P,G,x)=a)]\n\nP-FDT:\n\nπ^* = argmax_π E[V | do(P-FDT(P,G)=π)], action = π(x).\n\nMaybe if we condition on x? Need decide. We can define policy selection before observation: π^* = argmax E[V | do(S=π)]. If policy selected after x but still policy over possible inputs, should it condition on x for exogenous info? Source says FDT responds to observation by changing intervention node, not conditionalization. But actual observations can carry information not subjunctively dependent on policy; P-FDT may need to condition on \"policy-independent\" evidence. There is unresolved. Need propose: split observation into policy-independent evidence E and policy-dependent observation X. P-FDT can condition on E but not on X when X is an output of the situation that the policy influences. In practice, use the policy node's input as given but evaluate policy's expected utility conditional on policy-independent observations. For simple examples, assume no policy-independent observations or use prior. Need mention uncertainty.\n\nNeed examples:\n\n1. Newcomb: policies trivial mapping no observation to one/two-box. P-FDT EU(one-box) = 0.99*1,000,000 + 0.01*0 = 990k; EU(two-box)=0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1000=11k; choose one-box.\n\n2. Smoking lesion: policy maps to smoke/refrain. Lesion not subjunctively dependent on policy; P-FDT holds lesion probability fixed. Smoking adds 1k if agent likes smoking; choose smoke. Need maybe with utilities: if lesion probability p, cancer etc. But simpler.\n\n3. Transparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker: Policy π maps observation full to one-box, empty to two-box (or maybe one-box? Need calculate). Predictor fills iff predicts π(full)=onebox. P-FDT chooses policy with π(full)=onebox because ex ante high EU. When actual full, one-box. Need calculate with 99% accuracy. If policy π_O: full→one, empty→two maybe. If predictor accurate, sees full and gets 1M; if inaccurate sees empty and gets maybe 1k if two. EU ~0.99*1M +0.01*1k=990,010. If policy π_T: full→two, empty→two? Predictor predicts two, box empty; if inaccurate full, gets 1,001,000? EU ~0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1,000 = 20? Wait source says $11,000? For transparent maybe they got $11,000 + q*990? Need not exact. Could present approximate: one-box policy yields ~$990k, two-box policy yields ~$11k, so one-box. But if sees empty, policy maybe two-box? Source's Fiona's q prior two-box upon empty; correct q=1. So policy: one-box on full, two-box on empty. For policy where two-box on full, predictor doesn't fill; if inaccurate and full, two-box yields 1,001,000 with 1%? Actually predictor 99% accurate: if policy two-boxes on full, predictor predicts two-box and leaves empty. With 1% error, predicts one-box and fills; agent sees full and two-boxes, gets 1,001,000. If no error, sees empty, and if policy maybe two-box empty gets 1000. EU=0.01*1,001,000+0.99*1000=11,000? 10,010+990=11,000 yes. Good. So P-FDT choose π(full)=one, π(empty)=two.\n\n4. Multi-agent example to show policy importance: maybe \"Predictive Prisoner's Dilemma with policy predictor\"? But action-FDT would cooperate if predictor predicts action. Need a case where action-level FDT fails because it chooses action after observation, but policy-level succeeds by committing to contingent strategy. Could use \"Newcomb's problem with a threat/extortion after observation\": A predictor gives you a challenge: if it predicts your policy would pay a small cost in a rare branch, it creates a harmful situation; otherwise not. Need more game-theoretic: \"XOR blackmail\" is single-agent-ish but predictor. Policy-level clearly refuses because policy refuses if letter. Action-level also refuses according source. Need find multi-agent where policies matter: perhaps \"sequential extortion\": after receiving a threat, you choose whether to comply; if you comply, the threatener later makes another threat. A policy of never negotiating with extortion deters threats; evaluating each action locally might pay if threat already made. Source's mechanical blackmail action-FDT refuses by counterfactual observation difference. But in repeated/multi-agent blackmail, policy selection may be needed to avoid dynamic inconsistency.\n\nCould use simple game: \"Parfit's hitchhiker with a second rider\"? Hmm.\n\nFootnote says difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas; maybe because in single-agent problems, choosing policy or action yields same prescriptions if the policy node's output only affects current action. In multi-agent, other agents may choose policies too, and equilibria depend on policy selection. Need maybe discuss game-theoretic policy selection: In a game, each agent's P-FDT chooses a policy. If agents are symmetric or have models of each other's policy-selection functions, their policy choices are subjunctively interdependent. P-FDT can support cooperative equilibria by selecting policies that condition on predictions of others' policies. Example: two agents in prisoner's dilemma with transparent source code choose policies. P-FDT chooses cooperate if and only if it predicts other's policy cooperates; this can yield mutual cooperation. But action-level FDT could also cooperate if it sees same reasoning? Maybe policy allows \"conditional cooperation\" (e.g., cooperate if predictor indicates other will cooperate, defect otherwise) rather than unconditional action. Need formulate.\n\nCould create example: \"Policy Twin Prisoner's Dilemma with observation\": Agent observes a signal S that predictor generated: S=good iff predictor predicted agent's policy will cooperate; S=bad otherwise. Payoffs: if both cooperate, 1M; if one defects etc. If action-FDT after observing good might defect because given good, defection dominates? P-FDT chooses policy to cooperate when good, maybe defect when bad, ensuring good signal. This is analogous transparent Newcomb, which action-FDT handles? Hmm.\n\nNeed maybe illustrate unresolved: source does not specify how P-FDT differs mathematically in multi-agent dilemmas. My interpretation: policy node's value is a full strategy, and other agents' policy nodes may enter the graph. P-FDT chooses best response in policy space, potentially with logical correlations between policy-selection functions. In multi-agent settings, one may need to solve fixed points: each agent's policy is a function of its model of others' policies. P-FDT may choose policy via expected utility under intervention do(S_i=π_i), while treating others' policy nodes as variables that may subjunctively depend on S_i if they run same/similar algorithm or predict it. If no such dependence, treat as exogenous. Need discuss Nash-like? But P-FDT not game theory equilibrium; it's unilateral policy selection given beliefs. If multiple P-FDT agents with common knowledge, each may reason symmetrically and choose cooperative policy. Need be cautious.\n\nNeed include \"policy-selection passage in footnote 16\" primary source. Need maybe state that the source gives almost no formal details for P-FDT; only footnote. Thus essay reconstructs. Need distinguish: Source claims authors' preferred formulation chooses policies; claims difference only in multi-agent dilemmas; otherwise paper's action-level examples likely carry over. My additional assumptions: formal policy set, intervention on policy node, ex ante selection, tie-breaking, policy-independent evidence conditioning. Need mark clearly.\n\nNeed target word count 2400-3000. Need final around maybe 2700 words. Need include headings. Need maybe include brief references? User says including references and any other material, avoid lengthy bibliographies. Could cite source in text: Yudkowsky and Soares 2017? The paper authors: Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares. Title. arXiv:1710.05060v2. Need maybe one reference line. Since source supplied, no need full bib but can mention. Word count includes references. Need ensure not too long? Aim 2600 words. Let's plan structure:\n\nTitle: Policy Functional Decision Theory: Choosing the Best Output of One's Policy Function\n\n1. Introduction (~300): Decision theories CDT, EDT, FDT. P-FDT is policy-level version. Quote footnote. Essay's aim.\n\n2. From action selection to policy selection (~500): Main FDT treats decision as output of fixed function; P-FDT output is policy π: X→A. Definition equation. Difference from EDT/CDT. Self-reference. Observations as inputs not conditioning.\n\n3.subjunctive dependence and counterfactuals (~450): Explain dependencies. Policy intervention preserves dependencies on policy: predictors, copies, models. Lesions not. Counterpossible reasoning. Graph G and do operator. Equation.\n\n4. Applying P-FDT: step-by-step (~400): 1 model world, identify policy node, enumerate policies, compute EU under intervention, choose π*, execute π*(x). Maybe formula with trajectories. Discuss observations and conditioning.\n\n5. Examples (~700): Newcomb, Smoking lesion, Transparent Newcomb/Parfit. Calculations. Maybe multi-agent policy example (voting coalition or blackmail deterrence) to show policy aspect.\n\n6. Open questions and operational assumptions (~500): Source leaves unresolved: which policies, when selected, conditioning on observations, multi-agent fixed points, learning subjunctive graph, logical uncertainty. My proposals: updateless selection at earliest relevant point; condition only on policy-independent evidence; finite policy class; tie-breaking; in games model other policy nodes and symmetries. Distinguish.\n\n7. Conclusion (~150).\n\nNeed total maybe 3000? Let's write concise but detailed. Need ensure P-FDT name used consistently. Avoid calling assigned theory FDT except when quoting source or referring to source's broader FDT. Could say \"the paper calls the broader family FDT; the policy formulation I call P-FDT.\" In headings use P-FDT.\n\nNeed perhaps include equations in LaTeX. Need final in plain text but can include LaTeX. Need no markdown? Can use headings. Need likely final essay. Need word count estimate: Need maybe 2600 words. Let's draft mentally.\n\nPotential details:\n\nDefine:\n- Observations \\mathcal X, actions \\mathcal A, policies \\Pi=\\mathcal A^\\mathcal X.\n- World model M=(P,G). Variable S_{P-FDT} denotes output of P-FDT's policy-selection function for M. It might be written \\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}(\\underline M).\n- Intervention do(S=π).\n- Expected utility of policy:\nEU_{P-FDT}(π) = E[V | do(S=π)] = \\sum_v v P(V=v | do(S=π)).\n- Choice: π^* ∈ argmax_{π∈Π} EU(π). Actual action a^*=π^*(x).\nIf sequential: histories h∈H, π:H→A, trajectories induced.\n\nCompare:\nEDT: argmax_a E[V | X=x,A=a]\nCDT: argmax_a E[V | do(A=a), X=x]\nAction-level FDT (source's main equation): argmax_a E[V | do(FDT(M,x)=a)]\nP-FDT: chooses π before applying x.\n\nNeed mention if observations are affected by policy, do(S=π) changes distribution of X. If actual x is observed, P-FDT does not simply condition on x because x may be evidence about policy output; instead, it treats x as the input to selected policy. If x contains policy-independent information, we can condition on it in P before evaluating policies. This is my operational addition.\n\nExamples calculations:\nNewcomb:\nΠ={one, two}. Predictor accuracy .99.\nEU(one)=.99*1,000,000 + .01*0 = 990,000 (or if one-box only box B; if predictor inaccurate empty get 0). EU(two)=.01*1,001,000 + .99*1,000 = 11,000. Choose one.\n\nSmoking lesion:\nLet p lesion. Policy smoke/refrain. Smoking doesn't affect lesion/cancer; adds 1000 if no lesion? Source had utilities: smoking utility 1000; no cancer 1M. With lesion fatal cancer 99%; no lesion cancer 1%. Actually expected utility difference smoke vs refrain = probability the policy controls action? Source had p? Simpler: If lesion probability q, cancer probabilities; smoking can't affect cancer, so expected cancer term same; smoking adds 1000. P-FDT smokes. Could mention formal: EU(smoke)-EU(refrain)=1000 * P(policy relevant?) but positive. In source, with uncertainty about utility function, difference p*$1000. Need not overcomplicate.\n\nTransparent Newcomb:\nPolicies: π_C: full→one, empty→two; π_D: full→two, empty→two. Predictor fills iff predicts π(full)=one (99%). EU(π_C)=0.99*1,000,000 +0.01*1,000 = 990,010. EU(π_D)=0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1,000 = 11,000. Choose π_C; if actual full, one-box. This also Parfit's hitchhiker: policy pay if in city; driver predicts policy; if pay policy gets ride. Need maybe pay policy EU: if promise/pay policy, 99% saved minus 1k; if refuse, 1% saved? Actually not necessary.\n\nMulti-agent example: Need choose one. Let's construct \"Policy Blackmail Game\" or \"voting coalition\". Source mentions voting coalition: ten agents each pay 1000 to vote, get 1M iff all vote. CDT none vote; FDT vote because decisions not subjunctively independent. P-FDT as policy: choose policy \"vote when in voting situation\". In multi-agent, each agent's policy selection may depend on others' policy selections. But action-level also would vote? Maybe P-FDT emphasizes policy across similar future situations. Could use this as illustration of policy but not difference.\n\nNeed maybe show policy matters: \"Sequential extortion\": Agent faces possible demand: Predictor sends demand if predicts policy pays. If pays, gets -1000; if refuses after demand, loses -1M if demand was credible? Actually mechanical blackmail. Policy version: choose policy refuse; predictor doesn't send; ex ante no blackmail. Action version could still refuse by counterfactual. But in multi-agent repeated, policy is clearer.\n\nMaybe use \"policy-dependent threat\": A rival AI can create a costly threat only if it predicts your policy will concede when threatened. If it predicts your policy refuses, it doesn't threaten. P-FDT chooses refuse policy; action-level after threat might have incentive to concede if threat already created and no future/predictor effects. In a one-shot after threat realized, action-level CDT/EDT concede; P-FDT still refuses because its policy was predicted. This is like mechanical blackmail; but P-FDT's policy node makes it explicit. Source's action-FDT refuses by imagining different observations, but footnote says policy formulation preferred. We can present as policy example and note source's action-level also gives same in single-agent blackmail, but policy version generalizes to repeated/multi-agent where local action evaluation would be unstable.\n\nNeed maybe include game where other player's policy depends on your policy's counterfactual behavior in off-equilibrium branch. Example: \"Mugging\": attacker demands wallet if predicts you would comply; if policy refuses, no attack. But if attacked, local action may comply to avoid harm. P-FDT chooses policy refuse; attackers avoid. This is like blackmail. Multi-agent: many potential attackers observe your policy. P-FDT deters. Good.\n\nNeed discuss difference in multi-agent: If several P-FDT agents interact, each may choose a policy that is a function of predictions of others. A policy can encode \"cooperate if I predict you cooperate, defect otherwise\". This is more expressive than choosing a single action. Source leaves exact game-theoretic solution concept open. My interpretation: P-FDT performs unilateral policy maximization given a model of other agents' policy nodes; when agents are logical copies/similar, interventions on one policy node may require updating beliefs about others' policy nodes due to subjunctive dependence. If no dependence, treat others' policies fixed. Need mention possible fixed point: π_i^* = argmax E[V_i | do(S_i=π_i)] where others' S_j may be correlated with S_i through shared algorithm. Could be akin to superrationality. Need not solve.\n\nNeed philosophical motivation: global perspective, choose best world where policy output differs. P-FDT avoids precommitment because policy is disposition. Value of information? P-FDT doesn't pay to avoid info because policy maps info to actions; information can change branch but policy selection accounts for predictor response. Need mention maybe.\n\nNeed be careful with quotation: footnote 16 says \"author's\" but source has \"author's\" maybe typo (should authors'). Need quote exactly? It says: \"As mentioned earlier, the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node \\FDT(-) to choose not an action but a \\emph{policy} which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action. The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell, so we have set that distinction aside in this \\paper for ease of exposition.\" Need maybe quote partial. Since source mechanical extraction includes \"author's\" apostrophe. Keep faithful if quote. Could avoid quoting author's if weird. But user says keep quotations faithful. Use quote with exact? We can quote: \"intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" Good.\n\nNeed mention \"P-FDT\" not in source; it's assigned name. Need state I use P-FDT for policy formulation; source calls it preferred formulation of FDT. Need maybe not call action-level \"FDT\" too much; maybe \"the action-level presentation in the paper\" or \"action-level FDT\". Since user says identify any other decision theory by distinct name. Action-level FDT is arguably same source theory but not assigned? Could call it \"action-level FDT\" distinct. Other theories: EDT, CDT.\n\nNeed include references minimal: Yudkowsky, E., & Soares, N. (2017). Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality. arXiv:1710.05060v2. Maybe mention Pearl, Joyce? Avoid lengthy bibliography. Since target includes references, one or two lines. Need maybe in-text citations.\n\nNeed ensure final is coherent essay, not answer to case. Let's start drafting. Need monitor word count. I can produce around 2800 words. Let's write in final. But before final, need think through formal details and possible pitfalls.\n\nPotential issue: The user's phrase \"policy functional decision theory (P-FDT)\" maybe they expect theory where \"policy-selection passage in footnote 16\" primary source. They might expect not just general FDT but policy version. Need emphasize that P-FDT selects policy rather than action. The main source's equations are action-level; we need adapt. Need not over-rely on main formalization? But use it to build P-FDT. Need explicitly state footnote is sparse; my equation is reconstruction.\n\nNeed maybe define \"policy functional\" as functional decision theory over policies. Could say \"functional\" because it intervenes on a mathematical function: the policy-selection function. The output of that function is itself a function (policy). This second-order function selection is P-FDT.\n\nLet's define:\nLet d be the agent's decision algorithm. In P-FDT, d(M) = π, where M=(P,G) is model. The agent then computes a=π(x). The normative rule: choose π such that if d(M) output π, the expected outcome is best. Formally:\n\nπ^* = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(d(M)=π)]\n\nand P-FDT action = π^*(x).\n\nThis captures footnote.\n\nNeed maybe address self-reference: d(M) refers to variable representing its own output; Kleene recursion theorem mentioned in source. P-FDT can contain variable for its own policy output. No need know actual value to intervene.\n\nNeed discuss \"policy inputs\": observations/histories. In static one-shot, policy is just action; in sequential, maps histories to actions. In transparent Newcomb, policy's input includes \"see full box\" or \"see empty box.\" In blackmail, input includes letter/no letter. P-FDT can choose how to act in branches that may not occur under the chosen policy. This is crucial: the value of a policy includes its effects on which branches occur, because predictors may respond to the policy. Thus P-FDT may choose a policy whose action in the actual branch seems locally dominated, because the policy's off-branch commitments affect predictors.\n\nNeed explain \"counterfactuals\" under policy intervention: For candidate π, imagine world where P-FDT function outputs π. Then all variables that are functions of that output—predictor's prediction, copy's policy, other agents' beliefs—change accordingly. Variables merely statistically correlated (lesion) stay fixed. Then run dynamics to get distribution over outcomes. This is like Pearl do on policy node.\n\nNeed mention if policy space huge/infinite, idealization. For finite problems enumerate; for real life approximate. This is operational.\n\nOpen questions:\n- When to select policy? Source says apply inputs after choosing policy; but if an observation arrives before explicit policy selection, does P-FDT condition? My proposal: P-FDT should select policies at the earliest point at which the relevant predictors could model it; if later, it should act as if the policy had been selected then, provided predictors are accurate. If new information genuinely independent of policy, update P and possibly re-evaluate policy, but preserve commitments where predictors depend on them. This is tricky.\n- Which observations are inputs? Need specify information set. Policy's domain should include all observations that the decision problem treats as possible inputs, not observations that are logically impossible under policy? But predictor error may create inconsistent observations. Need include them.\n- Multi-agent fixed points: source says difference matters in multi-agent but doesn't say how. Need propose: model each agent with policy node; if agents share P-FDT algorithm or have accurate models, introduce edges between policy nodes or common parent representing shared logical output. Then each agent's intervention may affect beliefs about others' policies. Need maybe caution no complete solution.\n- Learning subjunctive graph: source open problem. P-FDT inherits. Need assume G given.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"fairness\": Source claims FDT does well in fair dilemmas where outcomes depend on behavior not procedure. P-FDT likewise. But can be punished for being P-FDT. Not central.\n\nNeed ensure not overstate: Source's footnote says difference only matters in multi-agent so far as they can tell. So for single-agent examples, P-FDT's prescriptions likely match action-level FDT. I can say \"I assume\".\n\nNeed maybe mention \"policy-selection passage\" has \"author's preferred formulation\" but not fully formalized. So essay is interpretive. Need distinguish claims: \"The source claims...\" vs \"I add...\".\n\nNeed final style: mathematical essay, readable. Use sections. No appendices. Include conclusion. References one line.\n\nLet's draft final now in mind with approximate word count.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n# Policy Functional Decision Theory\n\nIntro:\n\"Functional decision theory\" ... The supplied paper defines FDT... Footnote 16 says preferred formulation chooses policy. I call this P-FDT. It asks: \"Which output of this policy-selection function would yield the best outcome?\" Output is policy. etc.\n\nSection: The object of choice.\nDefine decision problem: finite actions A, observations X. Policy π:X→A. Model M=(P,G). Variable S = P-FDT(M). P-FDT equation.\n\nNeed write equations with LaTeX. Since final plain text can include.\n\nEquation:\n\\[\n\\pi^* \\in \\arg\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi} \\mathbb{E}[V \\mid do(S=\\pi)].\n\\]\nThen actual action \\(a^*=\\pi^*(x)\\).\n\nCompare:\nEDT: \\argmax_a E[V|X=x,A=a]\nCDT: \\argmax_a E[V|do(A=a), X=x]\nAction-level FDT: \\argmax_a E[V|do(FDT(M,x)=a)]\nP-FDT: policy first.\n\nNeed note do operator on S not physical action. The graph G includes edges S→A (through policy and observation), S→predictor variables, etc. If X is endogenous, do(S=π) can change distribution of X.\n\nSection: Subjunctive dependence.\nExplain calculator analogy. Policy dependencies. Lesion. Counterpossibles.\n\nSection: Applying P-FDT.\nStep-by-step:\n1. Identify policy domain H (observation histories) and actions.\n2. Build model with policy node S. Include variables for predictors/copies that depend on S.\n3. For each π, perform intervention do(S=π); compute induced distribution over outcomes.\n4. Choose π maximizing EU; execute π(current history).\n\nMaybe equation for sequential EU:\n\\[\nEU(π)=\\sum_u P(u) U(T(π,u))\n\\]\nwhere T is trajectory.\n\nObservation handling: If x is input, don't condition on x if x is downstream of S. If x is policy-independent evidence, condition. Mark as proposal.\n\nSection: Examples.\nNewcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb. Include numbers.\n\nMaybe a subsection \"Why policies matter\".\nExample: \"Deterrence game\". Suppose a rival can impose cost C unless it predicts your policy will pay tribute. Paying costs T, not paying if threat executed costs C? Let's formulate:\n- Rival predicts policy π in a possible tribute situation.\n- If rival predicts π pays when threatened, it threatens (creates crisis) with small cost? If π refuses, no threat. If threat occurs and policy pays, agent loses $1,000; if threat occurs and refuses, maybe loses $100? Hmm need make rational local pay? Need local incentive to pay after threat: if threat already exists, paying avoids larger leak? Mechanical blackmail: if program running, paying loses 1k vs 1M if not. But P-FDT refuses because if policy refuses, program not run. This shows policy chooses off-equilibrium refusal. Could use this. But source action-FDT refuses. But as policy example it's okay.\n\nBetter multi-agent: \"voting coalition\" source mentions. Ten agents independently choose whether to pay $1,000 to vote; payoff $1,000,000 iff all ten vote. Each agent's individual action has negligible causal effect if others fixed. CDT doesn't vote. P-FDT: if agents share relevant decision situation, policy node outputs are subjunctively linked; choose policy \"vote\". EU: if all vote, net $999,000; if not, lose $1,000 maybe. Need this is multi-agent. But action-level FDT also? It would intervene on action? If action node not linked to others, CDT-like; but FDT action node linked by same reasoning? Source says FDT votes. P-FDT policy version: choose policy for all similar voting situations; policy interdependence. We can use to illustrate multi-agent policy but not necessarily difference.\n\nMaybe create \"conditional policy in game\": Two players can either cooperate or defect after receiving a signal. A predictor reveals signal to each based on their policy. P-FDT can choose a contingent policy: cooperate if signal=good, defect if bad. The action-level theory that chooses only after signal might defect on good signal. This is transparent Newcomb multi-player? Hmm.\n\nNeed not prove difference; source says difference only matters in multi-agent but doesn't specify. We can say \"The footnote does not give a multi-agent example. A natural way to see the issue is...\" and present a plausible one. Distinguish own proposal. Example: \"repeated blackmail with many predictors\" maybe multi-agent. If each potential blackmailer observes whether your policy refuses. A local action theory might pay one blackmailer because loss already possible; but if paying changes reputation/predictions of other agents, policy refusal dominates. P-FDT captures by policy node affecting many agents. Good.\n\nLet's craft example:\n\"Many potential extortionists each learn your decision procedure. Each will send a demand iff they predict your policy would pay. If one demand arrives, paying saves $1,000,000 disclosure cost at cost $1,000. A local CDT/EDT agent pays. An action-level FDT agent in the paper can refuse by imagining that refusing changes whether it received the letter. P-FDT makes this explicit: it chooses the policy π(letter)=refuse. The policy's value includes not being sent letters by any extortionist. In a population of agents who can inspect one another's policies, the policy node is the object that others predict; choosing π is the stable way to avoid extortion.\" Need maybe not overclaim difference with action-FDT. Could say \"in multi-agent or repeated settings, the policy formulation is what prevents drift: the agent does not re-evaluate the local action after the threat arrives; it executes a policy selected because of its global effects.\"\n\nNeed open questions: source leaves policy selection timing, domain, conditioning, multi-agent fixed points. Need propose.\n\nNeed conclusion.\n\nWord count: Need maybe 2700. Let's start writing in final with careful language. Ensure no analysis. But before final, maybe count rough: Intro 250, Object 500, Dependencies 400, Application 400, Examples 800, Open 500, Conclusion 150 = 3000. Good.\n\nNeed ensure no \"FDT\" alone as assigned theory? We can say \"the paper's FDT\" but use P-FDT for theory. In equations use \\mathrm{P\\text{-}FDT}. Need maybe in headings.\n\nLet's write final. Use quotes exact. Need include footnote quote maybe:\nFootnote 16: \"the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" I'll quote partial. Need maybe mention \"The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell\". Good.\n\nNeed ensure final not too short. Let's produce. We'll monitor word count roughly. final around maybe 2800. Need no bibliography lengthy. One reference.\n\nPotential issue: The instruction says \"Target approximately 2,400–3,000 words, including references and any other material.\" We need be within. My final maybe 3000. If too long? Let's aim 2600-2800. I'll write efficiently.\n\nLet's draft final text:\n\nTitle...\n\nParagraphs.\n\nNeed maybe include \"where source leaves important question open\". Include explicit \"The source leaves...\".\n\nLet's produce final.\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write substantive mathematical essay ~2400-3000 words explaining P-FDT, taking policy-selection passage in footnote 16 as primary source for P-FDT. Need use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples, identify other decision theories by distinct names. Keep quotations faithful. Explain best mathematical understanding and application. Where footnote leaves choices unresolved, explain uncertainties and additional assumptions. Distinguish source claims from own proposals. Need likely produce final essay only. Need think deeply.\n\nWe need parse source. Footnote 16: \"As mentioned earlier, the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action. The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell, so we have set that distinction aside in this paper for ease of exposition.\" Also earlier footnote 7: \"In the authors' preferred formalization of FDT, agents actually iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions. This makes a difference in certain multi-agent dilemmas, but will not make a difference in this paper.\"\n\nUser says: \"Take the policy-selection passage in footnote 16 of the supplied paper as the primary source for P-FDT in this assignment.\" So P-FDT stands for policy functional decision theory. Need explain P-FDT based on policy intervention rather than action intervention. Need maybe rename FDT to P-FDT? Use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, including headings/examples. Need identify other decision theories (CDT, EDT, maybe action-level FDT? Could call it action-FDT? But source calls FDT; user says use P-FDT consistently as assigned decision theory's name, and identify any other decision theory by distinct name. Need be careful: source's FDT in main text is action-intervention version? Footnote says preferred formulation intervenes to choose policy. We need treat P-FDT as policy version. We can refer to source's main-text FDT as \"action-level FDT\" or \"the paper's action-level presentation\" but maybe not call it FDT? Since assigned theory is P-FDT, we can say the source calls the general approach FDT but footnote's preferred formulation is policy-selection; I'll use P-FDT for that policy-level theory. Distinguish claims: source claims policy formulation differs in multi-agent dilemmas, but not elaborated. Need develop operational interpretation.\n\nNeed essay ~2400-3000 words including references and any material. We can include equations. Need central definitions, reasoning, implications. Need explain how theory evaluates alternative actions or policies, relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Where source leaves unresolved, explain uncertainty and propose operational interpretation. Need likely include examples: Newcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb, maybe multi-agent (matching pennies? prisoner's dilemma? voting coalition? game where policies matter). Need mathematical definition of P-FDT: choose policy π mapping observations/history to actions to maximize expected utility under intervention on node representing output of P-FDT function as policy. Then actual action = π(obs). Need compare with EDT, CDT. Need formalism: world model (P,G) with variables including policy-output variable Π = P-FDT(P,G) or π*, perhaps policy variable is mapping. Intervention do(Π=π) sets agent's behavior rule; observations may be upstream? Need careful: In action-FDT, observation changes intervention node: FDT(P,G,x) = argmax_a E[V | do(FDT(P,G,x)=a)]. In policy-FDT, maybe choose policy π: P-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(P-FDT(P,G)=π)] then execute π(x). Since policy maps all possible observations to actions, the intervention on policy can affect which observations occur? In mechanical blackmail, if policy refuses when letter, predictor doesn't send letter; actual observation letter then under policy-refuse has counterfactual lower probability of letter. But if actual observation is letter, do we still evaluate policy globally before conditioning on actual obs? Footnote says choose policy, then apply inputs to select action. It likely means updateless: select policy based on prior, not condition on current observation? The source's main action-FDT still changes intervention node by observation but imagines different observations counterfactually in mechanical blackmail? It says FDT says agents should entertain hypothesis that, if their action were different, they might have made different observations. In footnote 16 policy version likely resolves this by choosing policy at outset over observation-action mappings. Need define P-FDT as policy selection: at decision time, with model and maybe current observation, choose policy? If current observation known, selecting policy after observation may be equivalent to action selection for current observation unless policy commitments affect predictor's prior predictions? In transparent Newcomb, need choose policy for seeing full/empty before predictor predicts. If agent at observation full chooses policy for all possible observations, can one-box due to global policy. But if policy chosen only after seeing full? Hmm.\n\nNeed infer P-FDT from source. Footnote 16: \"the author's preferred formulation of FDT actually intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" So node FDT(-) output is a policy. Inputs? maybe P,G? It chooses policy; then agent applies her inputs (observations) to policy to select action. In graph, intervention on FDT node sets policy. The policy variable determines action given observation. If observations are endogenous and affected by predictor, the policy affects observations through predictor? For selection, we maximize expected utility over policies ex ante? Equation could be:\n\nP-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(Π = π)], where Π denotes P-FDT(P,G). Then given actual observation x, action = π(x).\n\nIf observations arrive sequentially, perhaps at each decision point choose policy over future observations? Or choose policy as soon as possible, before predictor? Need operational interpretation: P-FDT selects the policy at the earliest relevant point, not conditioned on actual observation except as input to policy. If no binding precommitment, the agent's disposition is the policy. At observation x, P-FDT executes π*(x), where π* chosen by global policy evaluation. If a new decision problem arises not covered by prior policy, P-FDT selects the policy that would have been optimal from an appropriate prior/updateless standpoint, maybe using current model but not conditioning on contingencies that are subjunctively affected by policy. Need discuss unresolved: Which observations are inputs? What if policy selection occurs after predictor? How to define equivalence classes of agents/policies? How to handle self-reference and logical uncertainty? Need propose reasonable operational interpretation: Use a decision situation's \"policy node\" representing the output of the agent's decision algorithm for that situation, mapping all observation histories that could arise under the situation to actions. Evaluate candidate policies by intervening on this node in a structural model that includes predictors/other agents as functions of the policy (or its outputs). Choose policy maximizing expected utility with respect to prior over exogenous variables. Then action is policy(current observation). If forced to choose after observation, still use policy selected by pre-observation/counterfactual evaluation; if model changes substantially, reselect policy but include dependencies of predictors on the policy-selection algorithm, not just current action. Need perhaps call this \"policy node\" and \"policy intervention.\"\n\nNeed keep quotations faithful if quote footnote. Could quote: \"intervenes on the node FDT(-) to choose not an action but a policy which maps inputs to actions, to which the agent then applies her inputs in order to select an action.\" Need perhaps quote \"The difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas so far as we can tell\". Also footnote 7: \"iterate over policies (mappings from observations to actions) rather than actions.\" Use P-FDT. Need not over quote.\n\nNeed mathematical precision: Let \\mathcal{X} observation histories, \\mathcal{A} actions, policies \\Pi = \\mathcal{X}\\to\\mathcal{A} (maybe finite). Let world model include exogenous variables U, observations X, action A=π(X), outcome O, utility V. The policy-selection node S outputs π. Intervention do(S=π) sets the agent's decision rule. The expected utility of policy π:\n\nEU^P(π) = E[V | do(S=π)]\n\nwhere expectation with respect to P under intervention. P-FDT chooses\n\nπ^* ∈ argmax_{π∈Π} EU^P(π),\n\nthen outputs a = π^*(x) for actual input x.\n\nIf the agent has already received x, a policy-level theory may still compare policies by their ex ante expected utility, not conditional on x, if predictors depend on the policy. However, sometimes actual x provides information about exogenous variables not affected by policy. Need decide if P-FDT conditions on x? Source action-FDT: FDT(P,G,obs) changes intervention node to FDT(P,G,obs), but doesn't condition? It says FDT responds to observation by changing which node it intervenes upon, not Bayesian conditionalization. In transparent Newcomb they evaluate FDT(P_f,G_f,full) with intervention on FDT(...,full), but expected utility calculation includes possibility that if output twobox, predictor predicted twobox and box empty, even though actual observed full. They do condition on Obs=full? There is a typo: E(V | do(FDTpgv{full}=act, Obs=full) maybe? They said evaluates FDT(Pf,Gf,full), finding act maximizing E(V | do(FDTpgv{full}=act, Obs=full). But in calculation, if output one-box and accurate, full; if inaccurate, empty and action determined by FDT(empty). They condition on actual Obs=full? If condition on Obs=full, intervention on FDT(full) affecting prediction might change probability of Obs? Need maybe they condition only as selecting node, not conditioning distribution? The text has \"Fiona does not react to observations by conditioning her distribution. Rather, she reacts to observations by switching which variable she intervenes on\". The displayed formula has Obs=full but maybe typo. Need interpret P-FDT: no conditioning on actual observation if observation is downstream of policy? But actual observation is input to policy; policy must be selected given input? Hmm.\n\nFor policy version: choose policy π; observation x is then an input to π. If actual observation is x, we choose π? If we maximize ex ante EU over all X, π may choose an action at x that is not locally optimal given x but supports favorable counterfactuals. Transparent Newcomb: policy π(full)=onebox, π(empty)=twobox maybe. Ex ante EU high. At actual full, execute onebox. If we selected policy after seeing full but only for current branch? Could choose π where π(full)=onebox and arbitrary elsewhere; predictor predicted policy? If predictor predicts policy, one-box still. But if policy selection occurs after box is filled, predictor can't depend on it? Yet source says one-box even when sees full because predictor predicted that agent would one-box upon seeing full. That requires policy/disposition existed before prediction. P-FDT should treat the agent's policy as the thing predicted, not selected after observation. Thus operational: policy selection is updateless or at earliest point before predictors; actual observation just indexes into policy.\n\nNeed discuss multi-agent difference. Footnote says policy matters in multi-agent dilemmas. Need construct example where action-level vs policy-level differ. Perhaps game where another agent predicts your policy, not just action? Or Newcomb with predictor predicts what you'd do in each state; policy can commit to respond to observations in a way that affects predictor's behavior. Multi-agent: matching pennies? If both choose policies, equilibrium? Need illustrate why policy is needed: In games, selecting an action at each information set can be time-inconsistent; policy selection can commit to strategies (e.g., in Prisoner's Dilemma against a copy, choose cooperate policy). But action-FDT also cooperates. Need a case where policy vs action FDT differ: maybe \"Newcomb with a threat\": predictor punishes if your policy would choose A in some unobserved counterfactual? Or multi-agent where other player's action depends on your entire strategy, not only current action. Example: an extortioner demands payment iff your policy would pay in a counterfactual branch; P-FDT can choose a policy of refusing in all branches, making extortion unprofitable. But action-FDT in blackmail already refuses by imagining policy-like? Footnote says difference only in multi-agent. Could be game-theoretic scenarios where multiple agents each choose policies that depend on each other's policies, e.g., bargaining, threat, commitment. Need not have exact source example; can develop plausible.\n\nNeed explain how P-FDT evaluates alternative actions or policies. It evaluates policies, not individual actions. For a given policy π, construct hypothetical world where decision function outputs π; all variables that subjunctively depend on that output (predictors, copies, other agents modeling you) are updated accordingly. Then compute expected utility. Choose π with highest EU. Actual action = π(obs). This means alternative actions are evaluated via policies: action a at observation x is assessed as part of π where π(x)=a and with specified behavior in other possible observations. If several policies share π(x)=a but differ elsewhere, their expected utilities can differ; P-FDT chooses best overall policy, not best local action. Thus actions inherit value from policy. Need perhaps define projection: The P-FDT action at x is π^*(x). If multiple optimal policies disagree at x, tie-breaking unresolved; propose choose any, perhaps arbitrary or lexicographic. Need discuss.\n\nNeed relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. Source's subjunctive dependence: systems computing same function have outputs logically constrained. P-FDT preserves decision-relevant subjunctive dependencies under policy intervention: predictors of the policy, copies of agent, other agents' models. It severs mere statistical correlations (lesion) and preserves causal downstream effects. Counterfactuals are counterpossible: if P-FDT output different policy, predictors would have predicted differently. Need need graph G encodes how changing policy node affects variables. Equation analogous to Pearl do but on policy variable. Need note uncertainty: no formal True operator, no method to learn subjunctive graph; source leaves unresolved. For P-FDT, even harder: how to represent policies, identify which function is \"this very policy-selection function,\" determine equivalence across agents, choose domain of observations. Need develop operational assumptions: modeler supplies structural graph with policy node; we assume finite observation/action spaces; logical uncertainty represented probabilistically; predictors are functions of policy node or its outputs; self-reference handled by fixed-point/quine. Need distinguish own proposal.\n\nNeed maybe define P-FDT equation:\n\nLet \\Phi be set of admissible policies. Let S be a variable denoting P-FDT(P,G) (or P-FDT(M)). Then\n\nP-FDT(P,G) = argmax_{π∈Φ} E[V | do(S=π)].\n\nIf observations are inputs, the chosen action after input x is\n\nA = P-FDT(P,G)(x).\n\nFor sequential decisions, policies map histories to actions; EU is sum over histories:\n\nEU(π) = \\sum_{u} P(u) V(τ_π(u)) perhaps where τ_π is trajectory induced by π and world dynamics under intervention.\n\nCould write:\n\nEU^P(π)=\\sum_{o,a,v} P(o,a,v | do(S=π)) U(v).\n\nNeed compare to EDT:\n\nEDT(P,x)=argmax_a E[V | X=x, A=a]\n\nCDT:\n\nCDT(P,G,x)=argmax_a E[V | do(A=a), X=x? maybe]\n\nAction-level FDT from source:\n\nFDT_act(P,G,x)=argmax_a E[V | do(FDT(P,G,x)=a)]\n\nP-FDT:\n\nπ^* = argmax_π E[V | do(P-FDT(P,G)=π)], action = π(x).\n\nMaybe if we condition on x? Need decide. We can define policy selection before observation: π^* = argmax E[V | do(S=π)]. If policy selected after x but still policy over possible inputs, should it condition on x for exogenous info? Source says FDT responds to observation by changing intervention node, not conditionalization. But actual observations can carry information not subjunctively dependent on policy; P-FDT may need to condition on \"policy-independent\" evidence. There is unresolved. Need propose: split observation into policy-independent evidence E and policy-dependent observation X. P-FDT can condition on E but not on X when X is an output of the situation that the policy influences. In practice, use the policy node's input as given but evaluate policy's expected utility conditional on policy-independent observations. For simple examples, assume no policy-independent observations or use prior. Need mention uncertainty.\n\nNeed examples:\n\n1. Newcomb: policies trivial mapping no observation to one/two-box. P-FDT EU(one-box) = 0.99*1,000,000 + 0.01*0 = 990k; EU(two-box)=0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1000=11k; choose one-box.\n\n2. Smoking lesion: policy maps to smoke/refrain. Lesion not subjunctively dependent on policy; P-FDT holds lesion probability fixed. Smoking adds 1k if agent likes smoking; choose smoke. Need maybe with utilities: if lesion probability p, cancer etc. But simpler.\n\n3. Transparent Newcomb / Parfit's hitchhiker: Policy π maps observation full to one-box, empty to two-box (or maybe one-box? Need calculate). Predictor fills iff predicts π(full)=onebox. P-FDT chooses policy with π(full)=onebox because ex ante high EU. When actual full, one-box. Need calculate with 99% accuracy. If policy π_O: full→one, empty→two maybe. If predictor accurate, sees full and gets 1M; if inaccurate sees empty and gets maybe 1k if two. EU ~0.99*1M +0.01*1k=990,010. If policy π_T: full→two, empty→two? Predictor predicts two, box empty; if inaccurate full, gets 1,001,000? EU ~0.01*1,001,000 +0.99*1,000 = 20? Wait source says $11,000? For transparent maybe they got $11,000 + q*990? Need not exact. Could present approximate: one-box policy yields ~$990k, two-box policy yields ~$11k, so one-box. But if sees empty, policy maybe two-box? Source's Fiona's q prior two-box upon empty; correct q=1. So policy: one-box on full, two-box on empty. For policy where two-box on full, predictor doesn't fill; if inaccurate and full, two-box yields 1,001,000 with 1%? Actually predictor 99% accurate: if policy two-boxes on full, predictor predicts two-box and leaves empty. With 1% error, predicts one-box and fills; agent sees full and two-boxes, gets 1,001,000. If no error, sees empty, and if policy maybe two-box empty gets 1000. EU=0.01*1,001,000+0.99*1000=11,000? 10,010+990=11,000 yes. Good. So P-FDT choose π(full)=one, π(empty)=two.\n\n4. Multi-agent example to show policy importance: maybe \"Predictive Prisoner's Dilemma with policy predictor\"? But action-FDT would cooperate if predictor predicts action. Need a case where action-level FDT fails because it chooses action after observation, but policy-level succeeds by committing to contingent strategy. Could use \"Newcomb's problem with a threat/extortion after observation\": A predictor gives you a challenge: if it predicts your policy would pay a small cost in a rare branch, it creates a harmful situation; otherwise not. Need more game-theoretic: \"XOR blackmail\" is single-agent-ish but predictor. Policy-level clearly refuses because policy refuses if letter. Action-level also refuses according source. Need find multi-agent where policies matter: perhaps \"sequential extortion\": after receiving a threat, you choose whether to comply; if you comply, the threatener later makes another threat. A policy of never negotiating with extortion deters threats;