count. I can produce around 2800 words. Let's write in final. But before final, need think through formal details and possible pitfalls.\n\nPotential issue: The user's phrase \"policy functional decision theory (P-FDT)\" maybe they expect theory where \"policy-selection passage in footnote 16\" primary source. They might expect not just general FDT but policy version. Need emphasize that P-FDT selects policy rather than action. The main source's equations are action-level; we need adapt. Need not over-rely on main formalization? But use it to build P-FDT. Need explicitly state footnote is sparse; my equation is reconstruction.\n\nNeed maybe define \"policy functional\" as functional decision theory over policies. Could say \"functional\" because it intervenes on a mathematical function: the policy-selection function. The output of that function is itself a function (policy). This second-order function selection is P-FDT.\n\nLet's define:\nLet d be the agent's decision algorithm. In P-FDT, d(M) = π, where M=(P,G) is model. The agent then computes a=π(x). The normative rule: choose π such that if d(M) output π, the expected outcome is best. Formally:\n\nπ^* = argmax_{π∈Π} E[V | do(d(M)=π)]\n\nand P-FDT action = π^*(x).\n\nThis captures footnote.\n\nNeed maybe address self-reference: d(M) refers to variable representing its own output; Kleene recursion theorem mentioned in source. P-FDT can contain variable for its own policy output. No need know actual value to intervene.\n\nNeed discuss \"policy inputs\": observations/histories. In static one-shot, policy is just action; in sequential, maps histories to actions. In transparent Newcomb, policy's input includes \"see full box\" or \"see empty box.\" In blackmail, input includes letter/no letter. P-FDT can choose how to act in branches that may not occur under the chosen policy. This is crucial: the value of a policy includes its effects on which branches occur, because predictors may respond to the policy. Thus P-FDT m