e open problem. P-FDT inherits. Need assume G given.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"fairness\": Source claims FDT does well in fair dilemmas where outcomes depend on behavior not procedure. P-FDT likewise. But can be punished for being P-FDT. Not central.\n\nNeed ensure not overstate: Source's footnote says difference only matters in multi-agent so far as they can tell. So for single-agent examples, P-FDT's prescriptions likely match action-level FDT. I can say \"I assume\".\n\nNeed maybe mention \"policy-selection passage\" has \"author's preferred formulation\" but not fully formalized. So essay is interpretive. Need distinguish claims: \"The source claims...\" vs \"I add...\".\n\nNeed final style: mathematical essay, readable. Use sections. No appendices. Include conclusion. References one line.\n\nLet's draft final now in mind with approximate word count.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n# Policy Functional Decision Theory\n\nIntro:\n\"Functional decision theory\" ... The supplied paper defines FDT... Footnote 16 says preferred formulation chooses policy. I call this P-FDT. It asks: \"Which output of this policy-selection function would yield the best outcome?\" Output is policy. etc.\n\nSection: The object of choice.\nDefine decision problem: finite actions A, observations X. Policy π:X→A. Model M=(P,G). Variable S = P-FDT(M). P-FDT equation.\n\nNeed write equations with LaTeX. Since final plain text can include.\n\nEquation:\n\\[\n\\pi^* \\in \\arg\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi} \\mathbb{E}[V \\mid do(S=\\pi)].\n\\]\nThen actual action \\(a^*=\\pi^*(x)\\).\n\nCompare:\nEDT: \\argmax_a E[V|X=x,A=a]\nCDT: \\argmax_a E[V|do(A=a), X=x]\nAction-level FDT: \\argmax_a E[V|do(FDT(M,x)=a)]\nP-FDT: policy first.\n\nNeed note do operator on S not physical action. The graph G includes edges S→A (through policy and observation), S→predictor variables, etc. If X is endogenous, do(S=π) can change distribution of X.\n\nSection: Subjunctive dependence.\nExplain calculator analogy. Policy dependencies. Lesion. Counterpossibles.\n\nSection: Applying P-FDT.\nStep-by-step:\n1. Identify policy domain H (observation histories) and actions.\n2. Build model with policy node S. Include variables for predictors/copies that depend on S.\n3. For each π, perform intervention do(S=π); compute induced distribution over outcomes.\n4. Choose π maximizing EU; execute π(current history).\n\nMaybe equation for sequential EU:\n\\[\nEU(π)=\\sum_u P(u) U(T(π,u))\n\\]\nwhere T is trajectory.\n\nObservation handling: If x is input, don't condition on x if x is downstream of S. If x is policy-independent evidence, condition. Mark as proposal.\n\nSection: Examples.\nNewcomb, smoking lesion, transparent Newcomb. Include numbers.\n\nMaybe a subsection \"Why policies matter\".\nExample: \"Deterrence game\". Suppose a rival can impose cost C unless it predicts your policy will pay tribute. Paying costs T, not paying if threat executed costs C? Let's formulate:\n- Rival predicts policy π in a possible tribute situation.\n- If rival predicts π pays when threatened, it threatens (creates crisis) with small cost? If π refuses, no threat. If threat occurs and policy pays, agent loses $1,000; if threat occurs and refuses, maybe loses $100? Hmm need make rational local pay? Need local incentive to pay after threat: if threat already exists, paying avoids larger leak? Mechanical blackmail: if program running, paying loses 1k vs 1M if not. But P-FDT refuses because if policy refuses, program not run. This shows policy chooses off-equilibrium refusal. Could use this. But source action-FDT refuses. But as policy example it's okay.\n\nBetter multi-agent: \"voting coalition\" source mentions. Ten agents independently choose whether to pay $1,000 to vote; payoff $1,000,000 iff all ten vote. Each agent's individual action has negligible causal effect if others fixed. CDT doesn't vote. P-FDT: if agents share relevant decision situation, policy node outputs are subjunctively linked; choose policy \"vote\". EU: if all vote, net $999,000; if not, lose $1,000 maybe. Need this is multi-agent. But action-level FDT also? It would intervene on action? If action node not linked to others, CDT-like; but FDT action node linked by same reasoning? Source says FDT votes. P-FDT policy version: choose policy for all similar voting situations; policy interdependence. We can use to illustrate multi-agent policy but not necessarily difference.\n\nMaybe create \"conditional policy in game\": Two players can either cooperate or defect after receiving a signal. A predictor reveals signal to each based on their policy. P-FDT can choose a contingent policy: cooperate if signal=good, defect if bad. The action-level theory that chooses only after signal might defect on good signal. This is transparent Newcomb multi-player? Hmm.\n\nNeed not prove difference; source says difference only matters in multi-agent but doesn't specify. We can say \"The footnote does not give a multi-agent example. A natural way to see the issue is...\" and present a plausible one. Distinguish own proposal. Example: \"repeated blackmail with many predictors\" maybe multi-agent. If each potential blackmailer observes whether your policy refuses. A local action theory might pay one blackmailer because loss already possible; but if paying changes reputation/predictions of other agents, policy refusal dominates. P-FDT captures by policy node affecting many agents. Good.\n\nLet's craft example:\n\"Many potential extortionists each learn your decision procedure. Each will send a demand iff they predict your policy would pay. If one demand arrives, paying saves $1,000,000 disclosure cost at cost $1,000. A local CDT/EDT agent pays. An action-level FDT agent in the paper can refuse by imagining that refusing changes whether it received the letter. P-FDT makes this explicit: it chooses the policy π(letter)=refuse. The policy's value includes not being sent letters by any extortionist. In a population of agents who can inspect o